	3. Responding to incidents
		§ Incident response policies help personnel identify and respond to incidents
		§ Security incident is an adverse event or series of events that can negatively affects the CIA of data systems.
		§ NIST SP 800-61 Computer Incident Handling Guide
		
		a. Incident Response Plan (IRP)
			§ IRP provides more detail than incident response policy.
			§ It provides organizations with formal, coordinated plan personnel can use 
			i. Definition of incident types
				§ Helps identify difference between event and actual incident
				§ May group incident types using specific category definitions like attacks, malware infection or data breach
			ii. Cyber-incident response teams (CIRT)
				§ It is comprised of employees with expertise in different areas.
				§ Often referred as Cyber-incident response team (CIRT) or security incident response team
				§ They have knowledge and skills to respond to an incident
			iii. Roles and responsibilities
				§ Many incident response plans identify specific roles for an incident response team along with their responsibilities e.g. network admin, security expert
			iv. Escalation
				§ After identifying an incident, personnel often need to escalate it.
				§ Can require a technician to inform his supervisor 
			v. Reporting requirements
				§ Depending on the severity of the incident, outlines who needs to be notified and when
			vi. Exercises
				§ One method of preparing for incident response is to perform exercises.
				§ NIST SP 800-84 "Guide to Test, Training, and Exercise Programs for IT Plans and Capabilities."
			
		b. Incident Response Process
			§ Common phases of incident response are:
			i. Preparation
				§ Occurs before incident and provides guidance to personnel on how to respond to an incident
				§ Involves establishing and maintaining an incident response plan and incident response procedures, and also procedures to prevent incidents.
			ii. Identification
				§ All events aren't security incidents
				§ Identify whether an event is incident or not
				§ e.g. false positive in IDS
			iii. Containment
				§ After identifying an incident, security personnel attempt to isolate or contain it. 
				§ This might include quarantining a device or removing it from the network.
				§ The goal is to prevent the problem from spreading to other areas or other computers in network.
			iv. Eradication
				§ After containing the incident, it's often necessary to remove components form the attack.
				§ e.g. disabling or deleting compromised accounts
			v. Recovery
				§ During the recovery process, administrator return all affected systems to normal operation and verify they are operating normally.
				§ This includes rebuilding from images, restoring from data backup, and installing updates, also if admin identified a vulnerability then remove it
			vi. Lessons Learned
				§ After personnel handle an incident, security personnel perform a lessons learned review.
				§ A review might indicate a need to provide additional training to users, or indicate a need to update response policy.
				§ Goal is to prevent future reoccurrence.
				
		c. Implementing Basic Forensic Procedures
			i. Order of Volatility
				§ Refers to the order in which you should collect evidence
				§ Cache memory (CPU and hard drive) > RAM > Swap file >Local disk > logs on remote disk drive > Archive media
				§ Collect form most volatile to least volatile.
				
			ii. Data Acquisition and Preservation of Evidence
				§ Capture System Image
					§ Disk capture, may be bit-by-bit
					§ dd command in linux
					§ Takes entire contents of the disk including system files, user files, and files marked for deletion but not overwritten.
					§ After capturing an image, experts create a copy and analyze the copy, keeping original copy safe and not modify it.
				§ Take Hashes
					§ Hashing provides integrity for captured images of both memory and disk drives
					§ Can take hash before and after capturing image to verify that the imaging process did not modify the drive contents.
				§ Network Traffic and Logs
					§ Investigation includes analysis of network traffic and available logs which might help investigators re-create events leading up to and during an incident.
				§ Capture Video
					§ Used as detective control during an investigation
					§ Provides reliable proof of person's location and activity.
				§ Record Time offset
					§ Identifies times on tape recordings rather than actual time
					§ When analyzing, it's important to understand these times are often based on an offset.
				§ Screenshots
					§ Pictures of what you can see displayed on a computer screen.
				§ Witness Interviews
					§ Provide first hand reports of what happened and when it happened
					§ Right questions should be asked as they might not know if information is valuable
					
			iii. Chain of custody
				§ It is a process that provides assurance that evidence has been controlled and handled properly after collection.
				§ Some uses chain of custody form which provides a record of every person who was in possession of a physical asset collected as evidence.
				§ Proper chain of custody process ensures that evidence presented in a court of law is the same evidence that security professionals collected.
				§ If evidence is not controlled, if someone modify, temper, then it won’t be admissible in court.
				
			iv. Legal Hold
				§ Refers to a court order to maintain different types of data as evidence.
				§ It should be clear what to store and for how long.
				§ Data retention policies apply here.
				
			v. Recovery of data
				§ Refers to restoring lost data like restoring a corrupt file form backup
				§ Formatted data can also be recovered by using some tools
				
			vi. Active Logging for Intelligence Gathering
				§ Organizations often engage in strategic intelligence or counterintelligence gathering by increasing the amount of data that they collect.
				§ When in attack, admin might increase logging capabilities.
				§ Active logging strategy increases the amount of logged data collected on routine basis
				
			vii. Track Man-Hours and Expense
				§ Including this data improves the accuracy of the cost values used in the quantitative risk assessment.
				
	4. Providing Training
		a. Role-Based Awareness Training
			§ It is targeted to personnel based on their roles.
			§ Data owner
				§ Needs to understand responsibilities related to data that they own, including data is correctly classified and labeled. 
				§ They often delegate day-to-day task to custodians
			§ System administrator
				§ Responsible for overall security of system.
				§ Often need technical training to understand s/w capabilities and vulnerabilities.
			§ System owner
				§ High-level executive and has overall responsibility for the system
				§ Owner is responsible for ensuring system admin have skills and knowledge to maintain them
			§ User
				§ Regula users
			§ Privileged user
				§ Any user with more rights and permissions than regular user
				§ Often require 2 accounts
				§ Need training on classification and labeling of data they handle
			§ Executive user
				§ Needs high-level briefings related to risks that organization faces along with information on organization's overall information security awareness program
				§ Additionally trained for whaling attacks
			§ Incident response team
				§ Needs detailed training on how to respond to incidents
		
		
		
		b. Continuing Education
			§ Can takes in many forms  like in-person, virtual classes, one-on-one or in classroom
		c. Training and Compliance Issues
			§ Often training is required to maintain compliance with existing laws, best practices and standards
			§ Often prevent wide range of incidents
			§ PCI DSS Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard
			
		d. Troubleshooting Personnel Issues
			§ The way to detect an insider threat depends on the activity
			§ DLP provides best method to detect these activities
			§ Audits and reviews can often detect insider threats too
			§ Training program reminds users of the policies can minimize incidents
			§ If policy violations are detected, management can take appropriate actions ranging from verbal counseling to termination
			§ Security personnel can take additional steps to raise the awareness of these incidents through training and awareness programs.
